Raycast-style row rendering for the beamenu launcher.

Adds bm_menu_set_rich_rows(), which swaps the classic one-string-per-row loop
for a row that draws: icon, title, muted subtitle, right-aligned accessory,
and a heading wherever the section changes. Highlight is a rounded pill rather
than a full-bleed rectangle.

The row is two text lines in one row_h slot rather than one: line 1 carries
the icon, title and accessory; line 2 carries the subtitle, full row width,
starting at the same x as the icon (not indented past it). The icon is
centred against line 1's half of the slot only, so it does not drift toward
the vertical middle of a now-taller two-line row. Title and subtitle sit at
fixed baseline fractions of row_h (~40% and ~78%) instead of one shared,
font-metric-centred baseline, so the split holds as row_h changes.

Icons decode through librsvg (SVG) and cairo's own PNG loader, covering what
XDG icon themes ship, and are cached per (path, size) because the menu
repaints on every keystroke. gdk-pixbuf is deliberately avoided: its formats
live in loader modules discovered at runtime via GDK_PIXBUF_MODULE_FILE, which
would make icon rendering depend on an environment variable reaching the
wrapper.

The rich path is a separate function rather than a branch inside the existing
loop: it assumes lines > 0 drawn downwards, which is all beamenu configures,
so none of the fixed-height / lines-up index arithmetic applies.

